{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01TKEvZXJ4xS16doHCJ78YNn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. I wrote `ai-engineering/answers/08-002-minimize-harmful-outputs-while-staying-useful.md` — the answer to **Q2: Minimize harmful outputs while staying useful?**\n\nKey content:\n- **Concept:** harm-helpfulness is a continuous calibration dial, not a binary switch\n- **Three-zone response model:** safe (full answer) / borderline (scoped partial + redirect) / harmful (graceful refusal with alternatives) — the middle zone is where the engineering work lives\n- **Five levers:** per-category harm taxonomy with separate classifier thresholds, scoped partial answers for borderline queries, intent routing (DistilBERT pre-classifier cuts over-refusal 30–50% on dual-use), graceful refusal design (never a dead end), continuous calibration via production monitoring\n- **Concrete example:** legal contract Q&A — over-refusal dropped from 12% to 1.8% by narrowing the taxonomy, adding intent detection, and switching to scoped answers\n- **Key pitfall:** using a single classifier threshold conflates Tier-1 harms (need 99%+ recall) with Tier-2 policy violations (need < 0.5% FP) — per-category tuning is required"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":277,"cache_read_input_tokens":78125,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":277,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e76e6450-7832-4de4-bf6a-2c7007aeb21b","uuid":"c2055790-04d9-4789-853e-4efac813d336"}
